## 10. Safe-area CSS kill flattens the site against both edges

**Symptom:** the page has no side gutters. Columns and cards touch the screen
edge, buttons look thin and squashed. Worst on the partner's own test site,
because that is the one laid out with `padding` on `body` / `#app`.

**Cause:** the injection zeroing the page's box model instead of the safe-area
variables. Two generations of this bug:

- `*{padding-top:0!important;padding-bottom:0!important}` — strips padding from
  every element on the page.
- The "fixed" version, which is just as destructive:
  `html,body,#__nuxt,#app,#root{padding-left:0!important;padding-right:0!important;margin-top:0!important}`.
  Named selectors instead of `*`, but those are precisely the elements sites
  build their gutters on.

**The rule is about which properties, not which selectors.** The window already
pads for the cutout in `applyInsets()`. All the CSS has to do is stop the page
adding a *second* inset of its own through `env(safe-area-inset-*)`.

**Fix:** zero the variables, and nothing else. `padding-left`, `padding-right`
and `margin` are never touched anywhere. `padding-top` only on chrome wrappers
that are known to add a status-bar offset:
```css
:root{--safe-area-inset-top:0px!important; --sat:0px!important; /* ...all vars */}
.gameview-mobile-header,.app-header{padding-top:0!important;}
```
Re-apply on `pushState`/`replaceState`/`popstate` and a 2500ms interval.

