## 28. Logcat filter that hides the answer

**Symptom:** hours spent on "attribution doesn't work" with no evidence either
way.

**Cause:** the log filter listed the router and the WebView but not the
attribution wrapper, the config client or `AppsFlyer_<version>`.

**Fix:** always include them. The AppsFlyer tag carries the SDK's own
telemetry — `init_to_fg`, `Sending first launch`, the GCD URL — which is what
identified #19b:
```powershell
adb logcat -c; adb logcat -s <Router>:V <Tracker>:V <ConfigClient>:V <Offline>:V AppsFlyer_6.17.6:V
```

---

## 30. Redirect retry restarts the chain instead of resuming it

**Symptom:** `ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS` fires two or three times in a row on the
same host and the page takes five seconds to appear. In the log:
`redirect loop, resuming attempt 1` … `attempt 2` … each ~2 s apart.

**Cause — two independent bugs that look like one:**

- The resume URL. The retry reloaded `lastMainFrameUrl`, but `onPageFinished`
  overwrites that field with the page that **settled**, so by the time the
  error fires it names the chain's entry point. Reloading it walks the same
  hops into the same loop and burns the budget for nothing. The hop trail needs
  its own field (`deepestHop`), written from both `shouldOverrideUrlLoading`
  and `onPageStarted` — `shouldOverrideUrlLoading` does not see every
  server-side 30x.
- The re-entrant load. `view.loadUrl()` called from inside `onReceivedError`
  reaches an engine still unwinding the failed navigation, which defers it.
  That deferral is the ~2 s between attempts. Post it instead:
  `view.postDelayed({ view.loadUrl(resumeAt) }, 60L)`.

**Fix:** resume from the deepest hop, post the load, and give the exhausted
budget somewhere to go — see #31.

---

## 31. Exhausted redirect budget is a dead end

**Symptom:** after the last retry the app just sits there. If a cover was
raised it stays until its own 20-second timeout; if not, the user is left
looking at Chromium's error page.

**Cause:** `ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS` (`-9`) is not in the network-error set
`{-2,-6,-7,-8,-11}`, so once `redirectRetries` hit the maximum, control fell
through `onReceivedError` and **nothing at all happened**. No offline screen,
no reload, no cover drop.

**Fix:** make the exhausted path explicit. Try the URL the config endpoint
named once — the chain is stuck but its entry point usually still resolves, and
the cookies picked up along the way are often what it was missing — and if that
fails too, drop the cover and hand the page back. Never leave a branch of
`onReceivedError` that does nothing.

---

## 32. Push URL is dropped when the app is in the background

**Symptom:** tapping a notification opens the WebView on the **previously
saved** page rather than the pushed link. Works when the app is in the
foreground, fails when it is backgrounded or killed — which is every real case.

**Cause:** the message carries a `notification` block. Firebase then draws the
notification itself whenever the app is not in the foreground, and
`PushRelay.onMessageReceived` is **never called** — so the intent the tap opens
is the plain launcher intent, carrying the `data` payload as raw string extras
(`url`, `link`). `WelcomePortal` read only its own extras, gated on a
`from_push` boolean that this intent does not have, so the URL vanished.

**Fix:** one resolver that accepts both shapes, used by `onCreate` **and**
`onNewIntent`:
```kotlin
val own = if (intent.getBooleanExtra(EXTRA_FROM_PUSH, false))
    intent.getStringExtra(EXTRA_PUSH_URL) else null
val raw = intent.getStringExtra("url") ?: intent.getStringExtra("link")
return (own ?: raw)?.trim()?.takeIf { it.isNotBlank() && UrlGuard.accepts(it) }
```
The NATIVE lock still applies after it, unchanged.

If the backend puts the link only inside the `notification` block rather than
in `data`, there is nothing to read — that is a backend fix, not an app one.

---

## 33. The loading cover is shown for things that do not need it

**Symptom:** the user reports "a stupid grey/black screen on every redirect".
They are right.

**Cause:** raising the cover in `onPageStarted` for *every* navigation. The
original intent — "the user only ever sees a finished page" — sounds correct
and is wrong in practice: the page they were already reading is a better thing
to look at than a scrim, and an affiliate chain fires several navigations per
click.

**Fix:** the cover earns its place in exactly one situation — the session's
first page, where the only thing underneath is an empty view. Gate it on a
`firstPageSettled` flag set in `onPageFinished`. Redirects, in-page navigation
and retries all resolve behind the live page.

**Trade-off to state out loud:** without a cover on the retry path, a redirect
loop can leave Chromium's error page visible between hops. With the posted
60 ms retry the window is normally too short to paint, but it is not zero. Do
not "solve" this by bringing the cover back — that is the bug this entry
describes.

---

## 34. rebrand.py leaves the manifest pointing at dead packages

**Symptom:** the rebrand reports success, the build passes, and the app dies on
launch with `ClassNotFoundException` on the launcher activity. Or the build
fails with an unresolved `@style/Theme.App.<something>`.

**Cause:** two blind spots in the renamer, both from matching only
fully-qualified names:

- The manifest names components **relative** to the namespace
  (`android:name=".startup.WelcomePortal"`). The sub-package pattern is
  anchored on the package root, so it never matched, and every gray component
  kept a package that no longer exists.
- `collect_targets()` listed `res/values/strings.xml` but not `themes.xml`.
  Theme names embed class tokens (`Theme.App.Fullscreen`), so the manifest
  reference was renamed and the style definition was not.

**Fix:** both are in `tools/rebrand.py` now — a second `re.sub` for
`android:name="."` and a `res/values*/*.xml` glob. **Verify after every
rebrand**, because the failure is silent at build time:
```powershell
rg -n 'android:name="\.' app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml   # packages must exist on disk
gradlew assembleDebug
```

---

## 35. Misc

- `Locale` import: use `java.util.Locale`, not `android.os.Locale`.
- `intArrayOf<Int>()` is invalid — write `intArrayOf()`.
- `applicationContext as AppEntry` requires `android:name=".startup.AppEntry"`
  on `<application>` in the manifest.
- Logs hidden behind `BuildConfig.DEBUG` may not show when debugging release-like
  builds — during bring-up log with `Log.i` and verbose request/response bodies
  in ReachDispatch/TrackingDispatch so the gray decision is traceable.

