## 37. GCD returns 400 forever — the devkey is in the wrong place (CRITICAL)

**Symptom:** `GCD HTTP 400` on every launch. Attribution ends up empty whenever
the SDK's own conversion callback fails, so the config endpoint is asked with
nothing to identify the install and answers `404 {"ok":false,"message":"No
data"}`. Paid installs land in the native game.

**Cause:** the direct GCD call sent the dev key as a bearer token:

```kotlin
// ❌ AppsFlyer does not read this header
.url("$base$bundleId?device_id=$uid")
.addHeader("Authorization", "Bearer $devKey")
```

AppsFlyer says so explicitly, and this is why the response body matters more
than the status code:

```json
{"error_reason":"The 'devkey' query parameter is not found","status_code":400}
```

**Fix:** the dev key is a query parameter. The documented shape is
`GET https://gcdsdk.appsflyer.com/install_data/v4.0/{app_id}?devkey=X&device_id=Y`.

```kotlin
.url("$base$bundleId?devkey=$key&device_id=$uid")
.get()
```

Log the URL with the key masked (`devkey=***`) and always log the **body**
alongside the status — a bare `GCD HTTP 400` cannot distinguish a malformed
request from an install AppsFlyer has not been told about yet, and those two
have completely different fixes. This bug hid behind that ambiguity for weeks:
the 400 was read as "the install isn't filed yet" (#19a) every time.

Note what makes it so quiet: the direct GCD call is only a fallback, so nothing
fails loudly. It just never recovers anything, and the launch proceeds with an
empty attribution as though AppsFlyer had genuinely never heard of the install.
